{"step_index":935,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T21:03:38Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T05:03:38+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:03:46+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T05:03:38+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:03:46+08:00\nThe search for \"javascript-obfuscator options high obfuscation controlFlowFlattening deadCodeInjection stringArrayEncoding rc4 splitStrings\" returned the following summary:\nWhen configuring **`javascript-obfuscator`**[1] with a **high obfuscation** preset combined with options like `controlFlowFlattening`, `deadCodeInjection`, `stringArrayEncoding`, `rc4`, and `splitStrings`, you are employing a maximally aggressive security posture[2][3]. \n\nThe configuration below outlines how these options work together, what they do, and a sample JSON configuration.\n\n---\n\n### Breakdown of the Options\n\n1. **`optionsPreset: 'high-obfuscation'`**[4][3]\n   * **What it does:** Applies a pre-packaged suite of heavy-duty obfuscation settings[5]. It automatically turns on features like identifier mangling, string array extraction, and self-defending mechanisms[2].\n\n2. **`controlFlowFlattening: true`** & **`controlFlowFlatteningThreshold: 1`**[2]\n   * **What it does:** Transforms your JavaScript code structure by turning blocks of code into massive switch-case statements wrapped inside infinite loops. \n   * **The Threshold (`1`):** Means **100%** of applicable functions are flattened. This drastically hinders human readability and static analysis, though it carries a heavy runtime performance penalty[6].\n\n3. **`deadCodeInjection: true`** & **`deadCodeInjectionThreshold: 1`**[2]\n   * **What it does:** Randomly injects chunks of dead code (blocks that look like valid operational statements but are never executed) into the script[6]. \n   * **The Threshold (`1`):** Maximizes the volume of fake code injected, bloating the file size significantly and confusing reverse-engineers trying to trace execution paths.\n\n4. **`stringArray: true`** & **`stringArrayThreshold: 1`**[2]\n   * **What it does:** Extracts literal strings out of your functions and places them into a centralized array[7][8]. Setting the threshold to `1` ensures that *every* string literal is pulled out of the code and hidden.\n\n5. **`stringArrayEncoding: ['rc4']`** (or `'rc4'`)[4][8]\n   * **What it does:** Encodes all strings inside the string array using the **RC4** symmetric encryption algorithm[9][8]. \n   * **Why it matters:** Unlike simple Base64 encoding[9] (which can be decoded instantly by looking at it), RC4 requires a runtime decryption routine and a dynamic key embedded in the script, making automated static scraping much harder.\n\n6. **`splitStrings: true`** & **`splitStringsChunkLength: 5`** (or default `10`)[8][3]\n   * **What it does:** Splits long string literals into smaller chunks and concatenates them back together at runtime using operators or array joins[8]. \n   * **Why it matters:** It prevents anyone from searching for recognizable whole-string signatures (like specific URLs, API keys, or error messages) using `grep` or text search tools.\n\n---\n\n### Example Configuration (`config.json`)\n\nIf you are using a configuration file or passing options programmatically via Node.js/CLI[9], a high-obfuscation setup utilizing these specific parameters looks like this:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"optionsPreset\": \"high-obfuscation\",\n  \"compact\": true,\n  \"controlFlowFlattening\": true,\n  \"controlFlowFlatteningThreshold\": 1,\n  \"deadCodeInjection\": true,\n  \"deadCodeInjectionThreshold\": 1,\n  \"splitStrings\": true,\n  \"splitStringsChunkLength\": 5,\n  \"stringArray\": true,\n  \"stringArrayEncoding\": [\"rc4\"],\n  \"stringArrayThreshold\": 1,\n  \"rotateStringArray\": true,\n  \"stringArrayShuffle\": true,\n  \"selfDefending\": true,\n  \"disableConsoleOutput\": true,\n  \"debugProtection\": true,\n  \"debugProtectionInterval\": 4000\n}\n```\n\n### Important Trade-offs to Keep in Mind\n* **Performance Impact:** Enabling `controlFlowFlattening` at `1` and `deadCodeInjection` at `1` will noticeably slow down script execution and increase CPU/memory overhead[6]. It is best suited for security-critical client-side logic (such as license validators or core algorithms) rather than massive application bundles[6].\n* **File Size Bloat:** Because of dead code injection, string array chunking, and control flow wrappers, your output file size can increase exponentially.\n\nSources:\n[1] [sourceforge.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFGiJwfr68cxLVryOfeqIHhtGG4M2e7AQBL0wQyPwrTIX5cSJxR72wu_8KDYC9gBDop11-UM9-x5-Ro_g9TVutqMw9vxLmM8aJj_8QQYfrTqW2X4jINBCs9nhTW9vLv72n9ZDA7DAV5)\n[2] [adminschoice.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF5PMfygNMBKxqjY28ZiwWWfMRqNVIrbqztWN-iKU4BIzNr2NifwkSQrKuHXBVSizbBvOlAEqFaH1vR3nGB6O2VIdpj7_EcdI0es4i_xlDmx8NxFI_p5wiNhje9za6roEFfylnjXhE8SzmuDXZvNB6uh9PQuRGGXrAe4aSt)\n[3] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG61tH33GF1sFHv5IQ0hO-hU4fZ8eorYE8JnGtLWDeqzntyis1w3vOIVg16eXR12IfoxHj4WzOrwiGbSO9ntSw5t3biyWBiw2vgS7qez1I6Ht1t11nTqxzRltt5HfevnY_Atn5VO5n9jYVTuWmBEStUQWeA8w-eiqx1QKAP1V0dvw==)\n[4] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHYYHFiPnhMHsjKmZPJfdXEsGd5XxAXhoJHHfZg9-ch9UZHr4BX_KoNC1bMqst9xd74hTupSljGWgR8AAEYaAMOk-1gukaVmlXaA-tw0otgTcktKjc2jKlVZ6DzQZPdIAuTiYZKIrxBAkOwUaJmDlucVYaBFWvSWRBIT0O9OvltAaR07mvzK9gY)\n[5] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE12ZVVizRuPneu6_cr11_x0LhntRFvXMO_09Q9xvMKTyTmXZvpnacTNJYChOBk617lwuAoTf4GAdsjd28tc0JA5zWwl1E88KMqFClq2TE93X7CMZVtCVu1AiKX4Gmbfede8KFOeKVlqljIX987Swf7Jtd4v-nfxfhclaY=)\n[6] [bytehide.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF_fwIUvhE5bHPsPieOS2bEYN9Ac_UCshEsDBCq7P_Qm1p2aMUwnW9MiEWLgjJ2Rt0AA_EY4PvKs1SPoFalB0Y9qIkthTyv0lcphhHq0-1ZLeD3k2IPDg-jZkitZsYfU