{"step_index":940,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T21:04:23Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T05:04:23+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:04:27+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T05:04:23+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:04:27+08:00\nThe search for \"javascript-obfuscator v4 JavaScriptObfuscator.obfuscate programmatic node api stringArrayCallsTransform\" returned the following summary:\nIn `javascript-obfuscator` (v4 and higher)[1], the programmatic Node.js API allows you to fine-tune how string arrays and their function calls are transformed. \n\nThe options **`stringArrayCallsTransform`** and **`stringArrayCallsTransformThreshold`** are crucial for masking the calls made to the string array, making it significantly harder for automated tools or analysts to reverse-engineer strings[2][3].\n\n### Node.js API Usage Example\n\nBelow is an example of how to implement `javascript-obfuscator` programmatically using these options:\n\n```javascript\nconst JavaScriptObfuscator = require('javascript-obfuscator');\n\nconst code = `\n    const greeting = \"Hello World\";\n    const message = \"Obfuscation is working successfully!\";\n    console.log(greeting, message);\n`;\n\nconst obfuscationResult = JavaScriptObfuscator.obfuscate(code, {\n    // 1. Must be enabled for the string array features to take effect\n    stringArray: true,\n    \n    // 2. Extracts a specific percentage of strings into the array (0 to 1)\n    stringArrayThreshold: 1, \n\n    // 3. Enables transforming/removing direct calls to the string array function \n    // and replacing them with specialized wrapper functions or direct inline decoders.\n    stringArrayCallsTransform: true,\n\n    // 4. Determines the proportion (0 to 1) of string array calls that will undergo this transformation\n    stringArrayCallsTransformThreshold: 0.75,\n\n    // Additional recommended settings to enhance string protection\n    stringArrayEncoding: ['base64'], // Options: ['none'], ['base64'], or ['rc4']\n    stringArrayRotate: true,\n    stringArrayShuffle: true,\n    compact: true\n});\n\nconsole.log(obfuscationResult.getObfuscatedCode());\n```\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Breakdown of Related Options\n\n* **`stringArrayCallsTransform`** (`boolean`, default: `false`): \n  Enables or disables the transformation of calls to the string array. By default, when strings are moved to an array, you see repetitive function calls like `_0x12ab('0x0')`. Enabling this option transforms those calls or routes them through hidden wrapper functions to conceal the lookup patterns.\n  \n* **`stringArrayCallsTransformThreshold`** (`number`, default: `0.5`, range: `0` to `1`): \n  Controls what percentage of string array calls will be transformed[2]. A value of `1` means *all* string array calls are transformed, whereas `0.5` means roughly half of them are randomized/masked, balancing performance and security.\n\n* **`stringArrayWrappersType`** (`string`, default: `'variable'`): \n  When `stringArrayCallsTransform` is active, you can configure how the wrapper functions are structured (e.g., `'variable'` or `'function'`)[1], making the helper logic blend into your code structure.\n\nSources:\n[1] [yarnpkg.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEaobMtxbiMI6Xucm1a9nLXfrS4FNYAHGfAIS78257KAQPqdnNlm4WNgspYtxe5VYfOkn7B01NUaDfwCLSRNtEStVFEhlmA4RoZRBRtFEiA6EYM_U84bc6s5xn1zMxgeo1vAjxgd_AvsWRsxaCOgOKgVw==)\n[2] [npmjs.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGpnKfR2CdGn7Ej_Huukb1No6TazIgAO_Z7npHzDieZKyKVbryMGfXIwSuALpkasCf-YA-1j9y0mCZ7y5bNsImG5Csenqp4EpIJ2RVgBvPM3WwIMSfLaP2psfTLYNt508VaWfQ3ytkejQ==)\n[3] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGINu3LF4zOWwU_MCgUFtPm_pdtWPvNOd-lUsYZ_NAr5WMFoPzLswCMXDIFyIJxgj9KW8QaVj9LZORo2DQmse3bBBHds27coTGR7tVOTp179yAMecOV6D7OO5wFX4wyUMJWwSwc1n9Ebxg-PNi1vqDloCctUbU1wbFzNzv6yVgq)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
